// recipe recorded below.
//
// BrnPostFxShader's composite is ONE draw through a twelve-permutation uber
// shader (BrnPostFxShader::Construct @0x823FDD18 makes twelve
// Shader::Construct(slot, alloc, VS, vsSize, PS, psSize) calls). This file
// carries ALL TWELVE PERMUTATIONS: one shared vertex program and twelve pixel
// programs. Bloom, the 2D tint and the vignette are in EVERY permutation and
// are switched off by their CONSTANTS, not by a shader swap; the three axes
// that DO swap the shader are the 3D colour-cube tint (+1), depth of field
// (+2) and camera motion blur (+4 cheap / +8 expensive).
//
// ONE VERTEX PROGRAM, AND THAT IS MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED. The twelve X360
// vertex packages (0x8203F0B8, 0x8203F630, 0x8203FB40, 0x82040180, 0x82040778,
// 0x82040E48, 0x820414D8, 0x82041C50, 0x82042380, 0x82042A50, 0x820430E0,
// 0x82043858) are 596 bytes each and BYTE-IDENTICAL -- md5
// a47e7e9943a3570c484e1724d6dff763 for all twelve. So there is exactly one
// gauPostFxCompositeVertexProgramPC and every one of the twelve
// Shader::Construct call sites is handed the same image.
//
// AUTHORED, NOT CONVERTED. Unlike the world material techniques, the post-fx
// programs are NOT in SHADERS.BNDL: they are Xenos microcode embedded in the
// guest image and handed straight to the program-buffer registrar, exactly
// like the sky dome pair in SkyDomeProgramsPC.cpp. Xenos microcode cannot run
// on D3D9 and no TUB HLSL source exists for it, so every program below was
// RECONSTRUCTED: each guest package was disassembled instruction by
// instruction with tools/assets/shaders/xenos.py and re-expressed as HLSL in
// tools/assets/shaders/brn_postfx_composite.fx, which is compiled twelve ways
// (the fxc /D switches are named per array below).
//
//   slot  E_SHADER_*                                X360 PS      PS B   PC image
//     0   BLOOM_VIGNETTE_TINT2D                     0x8203F888    696       976
//     1   BLOOM_TINT3D_VIGNETTE_TINT2D              0x8203F310    796      1072
//     2   DOF_BLOOM_VIGNETTE_TINT2D                 0x820403D8    928      1360
//     3   DOF_BLOOM_TINT3D_VIGNETTE_TINT2D          0x8203FD98    996      1472
//     4   BLUR_BLOOM_VIGNETTE_TINT2D                0x820410A0   1076      1600
//     5   BLUR_BLOOM_TINT3D_VIGNETTE_TINT2D         0x820409D0   1140      1680
//     6   BLUR_DOF_BLOOM_VIGNETTE_TINT2D            0x82041EA8   1240      1904
//     7   BLUR_DOF_BLOOM_TINT3D_VIGNETTE_TINT2D     0x82041730   1308      2016
//     8   BLURHQ_BLOOM_VIGNETTE_TINT2D              0x82042CA8   1076      1600
//     9   BLURHQ_BLOOM_TINT3D_VIGNETTE_TINT2D       0x820425D8   1140      1680
//    10   BLURHQ_DOF_BLOOM_VIGNETTE_TINT2D          0x82043AB0   1240      1904
//    11   BLURHQ_DOF_BLOOM_TINT3D_VIGNETTE_TINT2D   0x82043338   1308      2016
//
// The constant surface is not guessed -- it is the CTAB interned in each
// microcode package, and the PC build reproduces every one of the twelve
// register/type/count surfaces EXACTLY, name for name, with no orphan on
// either side (the .fx pins each constant with register(); the check is
// scratch/postfx_step5_wave/permutations/work/check_perms.py, whose output is
// pasted in the wave report). A name's register is NOT stable across
// permutations -- BloomColour is c1 without depth of field and c3 with it --
// which is why the engine resolves by NAME through
// renderengine::ProgramBuffer::GetVariableHandleByName and why nothing here
// depends on the numbering:
//     vertex   : c0 VignetteCentreXyScaleXy, c1 VignetteAngle
//     pixel    : c0 GlobalParams, [c1 DofParamsA, c2 DofParamsB,] BloomColour,
//                VignetteInnerRgbPlusMul, VignetteOuterRgbPlusAdd,
//                Tint2dColour, [BlurMatrixX, BlurMatrixY, BlurMatrixW]
//     samplers : s0 SamplerSource, s1 SamplerBloom, s2 SamplerDof,
//                s3 Sampler3dTint, s4 SamplerDepth
//
// WHAT THE COMPOSITE COMPUTES (recovered from the microcode; the full
// per-instruction mapping is in the wave report):
//     depth    = SamplerDepth                              // DOF or BLUR only
//     source   = tex2D(SamplerSource, uv).rgb              // no blur
//              | tex2Dgrad(SamplerSource, uv + velocity*jitter,
//                          velocity, perp(velocity)/256)   // BLUR / BLURHQ
//     source   = lerp(source, tex2D(SamplerDof, uv).rgb,   // DOF only
//                     saturate(max((DofParamsA.y - depth) * DofParamsB.y,
//                                  (depth - DofParamsA.z) * DofParamsB.z))
//                     * DofParamsB.x)
//     source  *= GlobalParams.x                            // 1/whiteLevel
//     bloom    = tex2D(SamplerBloom, uv).rgb * BloomColour.rgb
//     compose  = source + bloom - saturate(source * bloom) // SCREEN blend
//     compose  = tex3D(Sampler3dTint, compose).rgb         // TINT3D only
//     t        = saturate(length(vignetteUv) + VignetteOuterRgbPlusAdd.w)
//     gradient = t * t * (3 - 2 * t)                       // smoothstep
//     vignette = lerp(VignetteInnerRgbPlusMul.rgb,
//                     VignetteOuterRgbPlusAdd.rgb, gradient)
//     colour   = compose * vignette + Tint2dColour.rgb
// and the vertex program passes the clip-space quad through untransformed
// while building the vignette-space coordinate in TEXCOORD0.zw:
//     screen   = uv * 2 - 1
//     rotated  = rotate(screen, VignetteAngle.x)
//     vigUv    = (rotated - (VignetteCentreXyScaleXy.xy * 2 - 1))
//                * VignetteCentreXyScaleXy.zw
//
// THE MOTION BLUR IS ONE ANISOTROPIC FETCH, NOT A TAP LOOP -- and BLURHQ
// differs from BLUR by exactly ONE constant. The console sets the fetch unit's
// two gradient latches by hand (setGradientH = the screen velocity,
// setGradientV = its perpendicular scaled by 1/256) and then issues a single
// tfetch, so the sampler's ANISOTROPIC filter integrates the streak in
// hardware; that is why BrnPostFxShader::Construct builds a 4x and a 16x
// anisotropic sampler and Render binds mapSamplerState_MotionBlur[meQuality]
// on unit 0 exactly when blur is on. Diffing the console's four BLUR/BLURHQ
// pairs (4 vs 8, 5 vs 9, 6 vs 10, 7 vs 11) instruction by instruction leaves
// ONE changed value, the sub-tap jitter scale: 0.25 (== 1/4) for the cheap
// quality, 0.0625 (== 1/16) for the expensive one -- i.e. 1/MaxAnisotropy. The
// PC pairs reproduce that: comp_ps04.fxo and comp_ps08.fxo differ in exactly
// one byte, and so do 05/09, 06/10 and 07/11.
//
// TWO DISCLOSED PC DEVIATIONS, both in the .fx and both in the wave report:
//   * DEPTH ENCODING. The console decodes a 24-bit depth out of three colour
//     lanes of an A8R8G8B8 fetch of the D24S8 surface (weights 255/16777215,
//     65280/16777215, 16711680/16777215, byte-identical in all ten
//     permutations that read depth). A PC raw-depth texture (INTZ/DF24/DF16)
//     returns the depth VALUE in .r, so the PC programs read .r. The semantic
//     -- normalised device depth in [0,1] -- is identical. ⚠ Bound instead to
//     a D24X8 hardware-COMPARE depth texture, that fetch returns 0/1 and both
//     depth of field and motion blur read a binary mask.
//   * THE STENCIL MASK. The same console fetch's fourth lane is the STENCIL
//     byte, and the blur programs multiply the screen velocity by it -- a
//     per-pixel blur mask. D3D9 cannot sample a depth texture's stencil, so
//     the PC programs use 1.0.
//
// NEUTRAL (pass-through) CONSTANTS, i.e. what BrnPostFxShader::Render already
// writes with every effect bit clear, brightness 0, contrast 1, white level 1:
//     GlobalParams            = { 1, 0, 0, 0 }
//     BloomColour             = { 0, 0, 0, 0 }   -> the screen blend collapses
//                                                   to source
//     VignetteInnerRgbPlusMul = VignetteOuterRgbPlusAdd = { 1, 1, 1, * }
//                                                -> lerp of two equal colours
//                                                   is white for ANY gradient
//     Tint2dColour            = { 0, 0, 0, 0 }
//     VignetteAngle           = { 0, 0, 0, 0 }
//     VignetteCentreXyScaleXy = { 1, 1, 1, 1 }
// With those the composite is an exact pass-through of SamplerSource.
//
// Build recipe (the same flags convert_shaders_bundle.py::compile_entry uses,
// so these blobs match every other converted PC program; /Zpr (row-major
// packing) is load-bearing -- fxc's column-major default silently transposes
// every matrix constant):
//     fxc /nologo /T vs_3_0 /E mainVS /O2 /Zpr /Fo composite_vs.fxo brn_postfx_composite.fx
//     fxc /nologo /T ps_3_0 /E mainPS /O2 /Zpr <the /D switches named per
//         array below> /Fo composite_psNN.fxo brn_postfx_composite.fx
// then wrapped by tools/assets/shaders/shader_transcode.py ::
// build_pc_program_buffer(bytecode, shader_type) into platform-4
// ShaderProgramBuffer primary images (0x14-byte ProgramBufferData header +
// D3D9 bytecode at +0x14 + 8-byte ProgramVariableDescriptor table rebuilt from
// the bytecode's CTAB + interned NUL name strings). Only the primary is
// carried: muMicrocodePart3 is 0, there is no Xenos physical block. Adopt them
// with renderengine::ProgramBufferPC_Adopt (programbuffer.h:123), which copies
// the image into low-4 GB memory and rebases the descriptor name offsets so
// GetVariableHandleByName keeps working unchanged.
//
// ⚠ THE PERMUTATION-0 PAIR AND THE VERTEX IMAGE BELOW ARE THE 2026-08-14 BYTES,
// UNCHANGED. Restructuring the .fx into a twelve-way source could have moved
// the one program that is live today. It did not: re-running the recipe with no
// /D switches reproduces both shipped images BYTE FOR BYTE (516-byte vertex
// bytecode, 768-byte pixel bytecode, verified against these very arrays by
// work/check_perms.py section 1). Nothing about the live picture can move.
